= Present Situation

Streets are hot across Europe, and Barcelona is one of the epicenters of that story. After the first General Strike in a decade in 2010, the anti-austerity movement is growing and becoming a force for political change in its own right. Somewhere between 6 and 8 million protestors have joined the movement across Spain, demanding a stop to austerity and a break away from the PP-PSOE two-party system that has ruled the state since the death of Franco. 

In parallel to that, the failure to build achieve a new autonomy-based status quo for Catalonia has pushed many towards supporting independence. From a relatively minor political position for the last few decades, now the debate on the topic has already become the front and center of Catalan politics. The organizations behind the movement, spearheaded by the ANC and Omnium Cultural, already made a show of force in 2010, and from then they have only grown in supports and political influence. 

